course: books
lesson: 25
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter I, part 1: The River Bank (the Mole leaves his spring-cleaning and meets the Rat)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Mole has been spring-cleaning his little underground home all morning, until he is covered in dust and whitewash and fed up. Suddenly he throws down his brush, says «Hang spring-cleaning!» and digs his way up into the sunshine of a big meadow.
explain: He is so happy that he knocks over a fussy old rabbit who wants sixpence to let him pass, and runs on through the fields while everyone else is busy. He feels like the only one on holiday, and that is half the fun.
explain: Then he sees a river for the first time in his life. It seems alive — chattering, chuckling, sparkling — and he sits on the bank, spellbound, as if it were telling him stories.
explain: In a hole in the opposite bank something twinkles: an eye, then a whiskered brown face. It is the Water Rat. The Rat rows across in a little blue-and-white boat, helps the Mole in, and the Mole admits he has never been in a boat before.
explain: The Rat is amazed, and dreamily tells him there is nothing «half so much worth doing as simply messing about in boats» — and crashes into the bank while he says it. He just laughs, and invites the Mole to spend the whole day on the river with him.
fact: The book is «The Wind in the Willows» by Kenneth Grahame, first published in 1908; the animals talk, wear clothes and live in little houses, but still live like animals in many ways.
fact: The Mole was spring-cleaning with brooms, dusters and a pail of whitewash (a white paint for walls) till he had «dust in his throat and eyes» and an aching back.
fact: He threw down his brush and said «Bother!» and «O blow!» and «Hang spring-cleaning!» — all old, mild ways of saying «I've had enough!»
fact: He dug up his tunnel: «he scraped and scratched and scrabbled and scrooged», muttering to himself «Up we go! Up we go!», until «pop! his snout came out into the sunlight»; then, full of joy, he jumped «off all his four legs at once».
fact: An elderly rabbit at a gap in the hedge asked «Sixpence for the privilege of passing by the private road!» (sixpence = an old small coin; privilege = a special right); the Mole knocked him over and trotted on.
fact: The Mole shouted «Onion-sauce! Onion-sauce!» at the rabbits as a cheeky jeer; onion sauce was served with roast rabbit, so it was a rude joke. The rabbits only blamed each other once it was too late: «How stupid you are! Why didn't you tell him—».
fact: The book says: «the best part of a holiday is perhaps not so much to be resting yourself, as to see all the other fellows busy working.»
fact: The Mole had never seen a river before, and was «bewitched, entranced, fascinated»; it seemed to him «this sleek, sinuous, full-bodied animal, chasing and chuckling» (sinuous = winding like a snake).
fact: A twinkle in a hole in the far bank turned out to be an eye, then «A brown little face, with whiskers» — the Water Rat. (A «water rat» here is really a water vole, a gentle river animal.)
fact: When the Rat asks «Would you like to come over?», the Mole answers «rather pettishly» (a bit crossly) «Oh, it's all very well to talk», because he is new to the river and cannot see how to get across.
fact: The Rat's little boat was «painted blue outside and white within», «just the size for two animals»; he sculled (rowed with a pair of short oars, called sculls) across to fetch the Mole.
fact: «Do you know, I've never been in a boat before in all my life.» — the Rat was so shocked he could hardly finish a sentence.
fact: The Rat: «there is nothing—absolute nothing—half so much worth doing as simply messing about in boats.» While saying it he hit the bank and fell on his back with «his heels in the air», and then just picked himself up «with a pleasant laugh» and carried on talking calmly: «Nothing seems really to matter, that's the charm of it. Whether you get away, or whether you don't ... you're always busy, and you never do anything in particular.»
fact: Old words in this part: «hither and thither» = here and there; «gingerly» = very carefully; «rapture» = great joy; «pettishly» = a bit sulkily; «composedly» = calmly; «Hold up!» = stop!
fact: Talk in this lesson: «bruv» (London) = mate; «wee» (Scotland) = small; «sweet as» (New Zealand) = great; «lowkey» = a bit, quietly; «no cap» = honestly; «mardy» (Midlands) = sulky.
exercise: What was the Mole doing all morning at the start of the book? | Spring-cleaning his little home | Digging a new tunnel | Sleeping late in his warm bed | Cooking a big lunch
exercise: What was the Mole covered in by the time he stopped? | Dust and splashes of whitewash | Mud from the river bank | Flour from baking bread | Soot from the fireplace
exercise: What does the Mole say when he throws down his brush? | «Hang spring-cleaning!» | «Hooray for the spring!» | «Off with all the dust!» | «I'll finish it all tomorrow, I promise!»
exercise: How does the Mole get out of his house and up into the meadow? | He scrabbles up his steep little tunnel | He climbs a ladder up his chimney | He walks out of a front door | He is carried out by a bird
exercise: «pop! his snout came out into the sunlight». What is a snout? | An animal's nose and mouth | A small woollen hat for cold mornings | The front paw of a digging animal | A whisker
exercise: What does the elderly rabbit at the hedge ask the Mole for? | Sixpence to pass along the private road | A carrot from his garden | His name and address | Help with his cleaning
exercise: What does the Mole do to the elderly rabbit? | He bowls him over and trots on | He pays him the sixpence politely and thanks him | He runs home crying | He bows low
exercise: What does the Mole shout at the other rabbits? | «Onion-sauce! Onion-sauce!» | «Sixpence! Sixpence!» | «Up we go!» | «Look out, here I come, all of you!»
exercise: What do the rabbits do after the Mole has gone? | Grumble at each other about what they should have said | Chase him across the meadow and catch him by the hedge | Laugh and wave him goodbye | Go to sleep
exercise: Why does the Mole enjoy his day so much in the meadows? | He is idle while everyone else is busy | He has finished all his cleaning | He is hunting fat worms | He found his lost brush
exercise: What does the Mole find that he has never seen before in his life? | A river | A rabbit at a gap in a hedge | A meadow full of flowers and long grass | A singing bird
exercise: The river is «chasing and chuckling». What is the writer doing? | Describing the river as a playful animal | Saying the river is full of fish that laugh out loud | Warning that rivers are dangerous to small animals | Telling a joke about otters
exercise: What does the Mole see first in the hole in the opposite bank? | Something bright twinkling | A little blue-and-white boat tied to a rope | A big brown paw waving at him | Smoke
exercise: Who lives in the hole across the river? | The Water Rat | The old Badger | The sleek Otter | The elderly rabbit who wanted sixpence
exercise: What colours is the Rat's boat? | Blue outside and white within | Red, with yellow wheels | All green | White outside and blue inside, with gold oars
exercise: How does the Mole get across the river? | The Rat sculls over and fetches him | He swims across by himself | He walks over a stone bridge | He jumps from stone to stone
exercise: Why is the Rat so shocked? | The Mole has never been in a boat | The Mole knocked over a rabbit who only wanted sixpence | The Mole is covered in whitewash | The Mole forgot his name
exercise: «nothing ... half so much worth doing as simply messing about in boats.» What does «messing about» mean here? | Happily spending time with no fixed plan | Making the boat dirty, wet and untidy with mud | Fighting | Fixing the boat with tools and paint
exercise: What happens while the Rat is dreamily talking about boats? | The boat hits the bank and he falls on his back | The boat sinks with both of them in it | It starts to rain | The Mole falls asleep
exercise: What does the Rat do after the crash? | He laughs and carries on talking | He shouts at the Mole for not looking where they were going | He cries | He rows straight home in a huff
exercise: What does the Rat invite the Mole to do at the end of this part? | Spend a long day on the river | Come and live with him in the Wild Wood for the winter | Help him paint his boat | Visit the rabbits
exercise: «the Mole stepped gingerly down». What does «gingerly» mean? | Very carefully | Very fast, with a jump | Proudly, with his nose in the air | Angrily, in a huff
test: Jayden says: «the Mole loved spring-cleaning, he finished it all and went out for a reward, no cap.» (no cap = honestly) Put it right. | He got fed up and left it unfinished | Right: he finished every single room first and then went out | He never even started cleaning | He cleaned the Rat's house
test: Why is «Onion-sauce!» a cheeky thing to shout at rabbits? | It was eaten with roast rabbit | It is what rabbits call their favourite spring food in the meadow | It's the Mole's own funny nickname | It's a very rude rabbit swear word
test: The rabbits shout «How stupid you are! Why didn't you tell him—» after the Mole has gone. What is the writer laughing at? | Arguing about what to do when it's too late | Rabbits who can't count up to sixpence | Moles who can't dig straight | Rivers that never stop talking to everybody
test: «the best part of a holiday is perhaps not so much to be resting yourself, as to see all the other fellows busy working.» Is the writer being serious? | Not really: it's a cheeky joke that's a bit true | Yes: it's a rule about holidays from a law book | No: he means the Mole hates holidays | Yes: you must work on holiday
test: Lily, lowkey mardy (a bit sulky): «the Mole had lived by the river all his life, so what's the big deal.» Is she right? | No: he'd never seen a river before | Yes: he was born on the river bank and swam in it every single day | Yes: he lived in a boat | No: he lived by the sea
test: How does the Rat's face appear to the Mole bit by bit? | A twinkle, an eye, then a face round it | All at once, jumping out of the water with a splash and a shout | Ears first, then nose | His tail, then the rest of him
test: What would have happened if the Rat hadn't had a boat? | The Mole would have stayed on his own bank | The Mole would have flown over the river on a friendly bird's back | The river would have dried up | The Rat would have built a bridge
test: The Mole says «Oh, it's all very well to talk» rather pettishly when the Rat asks him over. Why is he a bit cross? | He can't see how he could get across | He doesn't like rats at all | He is tired of cleaning | He wants sixpence first
test: The Rat crashes into the bank while praising boats. Why is that funny? | He's praising boats while steering badly | He is the best rower on the river and has never crashed before, so it's a shock | The bank moved | The Mole was steering
test: «Whether you get away, or whether you don't ... you're always busy, and you never do anything in particular.» What is the Rat saying? | Getting somewhere doesn't matter; the fun does | You must always reach the place you planned, or it's wasted | Boating is hard and boring | Only rich animals can go boating
test: Finn, wee (small) brother of Isla, says the Rat was angry at the Mole after the crash. Put it right. | He got up «with a pleasant laugh» | Right: he shouted at the Mole and sent him straight back to his own bank | The Mole was angry instead | They both cried
test: Which word list is the sound of the Mole digging? | «scraped and scratched and scrabbled and scrooged» | «chasing and chuckling» | «glints and gleams and sparkles, rustle and swirl, chatter and bubble» | «Bother! O blow!»
test: How do we know the Mole is happy when he first comes up into the meadow? | He jumps off all four legs at once | He sits down on the grass and cries with relief at the light | He goes straight back down into his hole | He starts whitewashing the green grass
test: ★★★ Count the times the Mole says «Up we go!» in: muttering to himself, «Up we go! Up we go!» How many? | 2 | 1 | 3 | 4
test: ★★★ Alice (lessons 1–24) also starts her adventure on a bank. How is the Mole's start different? | Alice goes down a hole after a Rabbit; the Mole comes up out of one | Both go down rabbit-holes deep into the ground and fall a long way | The Mole falls asleep and dreams it all, like Alice | Alice meets a Water Rat
test: ★★★ «Sixpence for the privilege of passing by the private road!» Who is in the wrong, as we'd see it today? | Both a bit | Only the Mole, as the road really was private and sixpence was a fair price | Nobody at all | Only the rabbits who grumbled afterwards
exam: Identify the occupation in which the Mole was engaged at the commencement of the narrative. | The spring-cleaning of his dwelling | The construction of a boat for the river | The study of the river | The preparation of a luncheon
exam: Ascertain the consideration demanded by the elderly rabbit in respect of passage along the private road. | Sixpence | A luncheon-basket | An apology | A fresh carrot
exam: It is given that the Mole had not previously beheld a river. Select the response which most accurately characterises his reaction upon first doing so. | He was bewitched and entranced | He was afraid and fled home to his tunnel | He was indifferent | He was angry at the noise
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the Rat's declaration upon the merits of boats? | He praised messing about in boats and struck the bank whilst so doing | He held that boating was a waste of time | He declined to take the Mole aboard | He capsized mid-river
